---
import ArticleLayout from "../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import PullQuote from "../../../components/editorial/PullQuote.astro";
import StrategicTakeaways from "../../../components/editorial/StrategicTakeaways.astro";
import { isLanguage, type Language } from "../../../config/i18n";
import { isCategory, type CategorySlug } from "../../../config/categories";
import {
  articles,
  getArticleByRoute,
  getArticleTranslations,
} from "../../../data/articles";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return articles
    .filter((article) => article.published)
    .map((article) => ({
      params: {
        lang: article.language,
        category: article.category,
        slug: article.slug,
      },
    }));
}

const langParam = Astro.params.lang;
const categoryParam = Astro.params.category;
const slugParam = Astro.params.slug;

if (!isLanguage(langParam) || !isCategory(categoryParam) || !slugParam) {
  return Astro.redirect("/en/");
}

const lang: Language = langParam;
const category: CategorySlug = categoryParam;

const article = getArticleByRoute(lang, category, slugParam);

if (!article) {
  return Astro.redirect("/404");
}

const languageLinks = getArticleTranslations(article);
---

<ArticleLayout
  lang={lang}
  title={article.title}
  subtitle={article.subtitle}
  description={article.description}
  category={article.category}
  date={article.date}
  readingTime={article.readingTime}
  languageLinks={languageLinks}
>
  <p>
    The Chinese AI market is not only a technology story. It is a capital market
    signal. It shows how investors, regulators, and industrial actors begin to
    price the strategic value of artificial intelligence before the crisis
    becomes visible.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this environment, AI is not treated merely as a software category. It is
    treated as infrastructure: capital-intensive, politically sensitive, and
    increasingly tied to national competitiveness, market structure, and
    governance pressure.
  </p>

  <PullQuote
    quote="AI markets do not only price growth. They price control, dependency, and strategic optionality."
  />

  <h2>Capital is reading AI before the crisis</h2>

  <p>
    The most important signal is not hype. It is the willingness of capital to
    fund AI companies before their business models are fully stabilized. That
    willingness reveals how markets value optionality under uncertainty.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chinese AI companies expose this dynamic with particular clarity. Their
    trajectory shows that the market is not only asking whether AI will generate
    revenue. It is asking who will control the infrastructure, who will absorb
    the cost, and who will retain strategic flexibility once the market
    consolidates.
  </p>

  <StrategicTakeaways
    items={[
      "AI should be analyzed as infrastructure, not only as software.",
      "Capital intensity is becoming a strategic filter for AI winners.",
      "Governance pressure will increasingly shape AI market structure.",
      "The market is already repricing AI assumptions before a visible crisis.",
    ]}
  />

  <h2>What this reveals</h2>

  <p>
    The Chinese AI market reveals a broader strategic pattern: when a technology
    becomes systemic, valuation starts to include more than growth. It includes
    resilience, dependency, control, and the cost of maintaining optionality.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why AI cannot be understood only through product demos, benchmark
    races, or short-term revenue expectations. The deeper question is whether
    firms, investors, and states can govern the infrastructure they are rushing
    to build.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
